  "text": "Question four. What is an agent, and how does tool calling work? Tool calling lets a model request an action instead of answering: it emits a structured call, a tool name and jay-son arguments, and the application runs it and puts the result back into the context. An agent is that loop, repeated until the model decides it's done. The Model Context Protocol, or MCP, is an open standard for exposing tools and data to models, so one integration serves many clients. Interviewers listen for the design around the loop: a budget on steps and cost, timeouts and retries for each tool, permissions scoped to the user, and a trace of every step. And idempotent tools, since a retried step can run twice. Filing the same ticket twice should still leave one ticket. Then reliability, which compounds. If each step succeeds ninety-five percent of the time, a ten-step task succeeds about sixty percent of the time. Make each step ninety-nine percent reliable, and ten steps reach ninety. Or cut the task to five steps, and it's seventy-seven. Fewer, more reliable steps beat a clever long chain. And an agent that acts is graded on what it did.",
